  "slug": "untitled-topic-af7b",
  "summary": "Recent simulation and theory studies link individual loss, movement and infection to changes in network structure and population outcomes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that network or spatial structure determines how changes in individual animals or hosts propagate to population and community outcomes. A simulation study across 268 empirical animal social networks finds that compensatory tie formation alters how individual loss reorganises social structure, so predicted consequences of losing an individual depend on whether remaining animals rewire. Two spatial studies connect topology to infection and herbivory. Host-parasite coevolution on spatial networks links degree heterogeneity to host resistance and spatial variation in parasite infectivity, and an individual-based herbivore model shows that movement and landscape structure jointly shape resource depletion and population dynamics. A theoretical model adds that parasite-impaired host mobility reduces virulence through altered relatedness and host density, with feedbacks on prevalence. What is new is the explicit coupling of movement or tie dynamics to evolutionary and demographic outcomes, although these items are largely simulation- or theory-based.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
